// corpus: known-failure spreading a lazy collection into a list literal keeps a lazy recipe instead of a list (ROADMAP: "A list literal does not snapshot a spread lazy collection")
// A list literal snapshots its elements, and `[...xs, k]` is the documented
// way to grow a list. When the spread operand is lazy (`take`, `drop`), the
// literal becomes a `ListJoin(Take(…), …)` recipe: it prints as a recipe,
// nests one level per loop turn, and does not compare equal to the list.
let ys = [1, 2, 3, 4]
let zs = [...take(ys, 1), 9, ...drop(ys, 2)]
(zs, zs == [1, 9, 3, 4])
// ➔ ([1, 9, 3, 4], True)
